klimadmin@altavista.com wrote:

> The problem is that a number of applications is giving me an
> 'unaligned trap' error and I have no idea, what is the problem.

See FAQ on http://www.alphalinux.org


> Although giving 'unaligned trap' message in a log, applications
> seems to work pretty well.

'Unaligned traps' are signs of a faulty programming loose with types.
It happens from time to time that they are indicators of other bugs but
one cannot be sure of that without careful analysis on a case by case
basis.  Although Linux kernel traps them and "fixes" each of those
traps, even if just a real "unaligned", is a performance hit.  Double
brownie points if you can fish them out and fix in C++ code.

If you are annoyed and do not care about performance and fixing you can
recompile your kernel and turn that particular 'printk' off.  It is
trivial application of 'grep'. :-)  The main reason for this printk was
to induce people to fix bugs - becase all these points are real bugs;
maybe not of a killer sort but still bugs.  BTW - they have a negative
performance impact on x86 processors as well but costs, due to a
different architecture, are considerably smaller.

> kdm
> kcontrol
> kpanel
> ... (most of KDE1 applications traps)

Figures.  Every time I tried to look inside of various KDE sources
I thought that I will puke.

  Michal


