Hartmann Schaffer <hs@paradise.nirvananet> wrote:
> you probably have a first generation alpha (21064?):  they have only
> full word load and store instructions.  halfword and byte instructions
> were added with later generations.

Nope.  A presence of new instructions in processors of a later
generation, which allow for a "native" access to smaller chunks of
memory, makes for more compact and faster machine code to get to these
(no need for stack games) but does not really loosen up alignment
requirements.  Fetching 'int' from an unaligned memory address will earn
you the same "unaligned trap" like before.  Maybe fixup routine can be
made faster.

  Michal


