 Alexander, you must be better at lock picking than I.  Making
 the circular paperclip tip was no problem (it was harder to 
 *find* a paperclip around here than to whip out the Leatherman
 and fashion the suggested tool).  However, this didn't seem to 
 set the pin depths to a common shear; so I abandonned that 
 approach in favor of brute force.

 Vicegrips applied to the body of the lock and judicisously twisted
 counter-clockwise (towards the "open padlock" icon which is
 impressed on the case) did the trick.  It did *not* result in
 breaking any parts inside (as one poster claimed it would).  

 With the case open I simply removed a couple of hex nuts, one
 allowing me to pull the latch assemble off the back of the lock and 
 the other allowing me to remove the whole lock from the case.

 No muss.  No fuss.  I didn't even scar the rest of the case.
 If I was so inclined I could wander to a locksmith and buy a
 replacement lock.

 Thanks.  I didn't realize that the interior end of this 
 formidable appearing Ace(TM) lock was attached to such a 
 cheesy locking mechanism.  Also the shiny chrome coating 
 belies the soft pot metal of the lock's body.   Luckily
 physical case security is not one of my requirements for 
 this device.

 (From what I've seen among PCs, SPARCS and now these Alphas 
 I'd not trust any OEM case for physical security --- I'd build
 my own cabinet for the whole unit!)

 Now all I have to do is find suitable SCSI CD ROM and disk drives
 for this think (and maybe find a bit more memory for it.  It has
 four empty SIMM slots.

 Any caveats there?

 (This thing has been sitting around the house for a few years
 waiting for me to get around to installing Debian on it.  It'll
 probably become a community accessible build host; for people
 to build and test their Debian packages or other free Linux software).



