>
>>>boot dka400.4.0.2001.0 -
>file /kernels/generic.gz -flags "root=/dev/scd0
>initrd=images/ramdisk.img"

Two things:
One - Since this is new to you, use the jumpstart cd first.  It makes 
things easier when you're a beginner, because there are a bunch of little 
details you can easily get hung up on if you're making decisions about 
partitioning on your own.  It also gives you some information you'll want 
for booting. 
Two - You can abbreviate almost everything on the SRM console, so use the 
following boot commands instead: 
(For RedHat 7.0) 
>>> b dka4 -fi kernels/vmlinux.gz -fl "initrd=/images/ramdisk.img text"
(For Jumpstart 1.1)
>>> b dka4 -fl 0

A little information about the stuff you saw when you did this command:
>>> sho boot*
The settings you see after you do this command are purely defaults, and
each one can be overridden manually.  For example, if you do this: 
>>> set boot_file kernels/vmlinux.gz
>>> set boot_osflags "initrd=/images/ramdisk.img console=ttyS0,9600
serial expert text" 
>>> set bootdef_dev dka4
>>> b
This has exactly the same result as doing this:
>>> b dka4 -fi kernels/vmlinux.gz -fl "initrd=/images/ramdisk.img
console=ttyS0,9600 serial expert text" 
Except that from now on, you can boot by simply typing the letter
>>> b

Next thing --
>>> set auto_action something-invalid
Your machine can halt on power-up, boot, or whatever else.  Don't make it
boot on power-up until you know you have your system ready for it.  The
command >>> b  Should work before you tell it to automatically boot. 

There is a lot of good information in the FAQs at various places.
Try these:
http://www.alphalinux.org/
http://www.tru64.org/
And although I don't see any FAQs, there are a lot of good links at
http://www.alphapowered.com/

Also, I am writing a FAQ for people who are new to Alphas.  It is not
finished yet, but keep watching this group for me to post a message,
hopefully by the end of the week. 


