machseven@hotmail.com wrote in <3a6df0ff.1768853@news.kuleuven.ac.be>:

>Hi, 
>I installed Mandrak 7.0 for Alpha without any major problems.
>The installation was succesfull accordingly to the message at the end of
>it. 
>
>Nevertheless I get following error during loading of the kernel when
>rebooting: 
>
>kmod: failed to exec /sbin/modprobe -s -k block-major-8, errno = 0
>VFS: Cannot open root device 08:01
>Kernel panic: VFS: Unable to mount root fs on 08:01
>
>Would this be caused by wrong mounting of the partitions ?
>(I'm sure the first partition on sca1 is the root partition)
>
>Your advice ?
>
>Thanx

Oh boy, I've got lots of responses in this thread coming up.  Many mistakes 
are happening...

Yes, this could absolutely be caused by wrong mounting of partitions.
The device "08:01" refers specifically to the first scsi hard disk, the 
first partition.  If you have your root fs on the second partition, for 
example, not the first, you could get this error.

If you have your / partition on the first partition, you should use sda1 
(or sca1, if that's correct.)  Otherwise, I suggest trial and error:  sda2, 
sda3 (or sca2 sca3, if that's correct, but I think it was type-o.)

If you are still having trouble, I'll point out that it's very easy to mess 
up the partition table on these machines.  You have to use fdisk, you have 
to use the bsd label (if you want it bootable), you cannot use the first 
cylinder, and it's easy to make a type-o.
If you want to be sure you do it right, get the Jumpstart CD.
The Jumpstart CD can be obtained at http://www.linuxalpha.compaq.com

Now, next message....


