NPB(NAS Parallel Benchmarking) Suite is real application benchmarks for
various computers. I tested NPB in alpha and intel clusters and the
price-performance ratio of intel is about twice than alpha.
You can see the results from

http://alpha-cluster.linuxone.co.kr/paper/NPB/npb-slide.html

and

http://alpha-cluster.linuxone.co.kr/cluster/npb/index.html

And my own CFD code for alpha & intel cpu results are as belows,

1) Alpha DS10 466Mhz/2MB/768MB 100Mhz Memory/9.1GB SCSI HDD

No. of PE :     1   Total Exec Time :    219.466 
STARTUP :      0.327 EXEC :    217.673 POST :      1.467   
    (%) :      0.149  (%) :     99.183  (%) :      0.668   

2) PIII 1GHz/256K/1GB 133Mhz Memory/IDE30GB

No. of PE :     1   Total Exec Time :    392.711 
STARTUP :      0.625 EXEC :    389.984 POST :      2.103   
    (%) :      0.159  (%) :     99.305  (%) :      0.535   

Above results are taken from 2 dimensional Navier-Stokes solver written
by
standard fortran77. In my experience the floating point performance of
Alpha
is about 2.5 times faster than the same clock Intel CPU's and 1.6~1.7
times
faster than Intel in interger performance.

> Hi,
> 
> I wonder what are your impressions regarding Alphas' performance,
> especially as compared to Intel machines. I have just purchased
> two Dual 21264 machines. One has 750MHz/8MB cache CPUs, the other
> 667/4.
> 
> Contrary to some reports I had got before I ordered them, I find both
> machines to be 2.5-4 times faster (per CPU) than Intel running at the
> same clock. I mean on "real" applications (not some specially tuned
> benchmarks) which in my case include period searches in data, photometry
> of stars done in CCD images of dense stellar fields, non-linear least
> squares fitting etc. All of these apps involve intensive floating point
> calculations.
> 
> Taking into account the price I paid for the Alphas, I'm a bit
> disappointed. The performance-per-dollar seems to be about twice lower
> for Alphas than for Intels (they are on average 3 times faster but about
> 6 times more expensive). Of course, the have the advantage of being
> 2 boxes replacement for 6 PC boxes, also I can perform a single task
> much faster than on a PC. Still, it would be nicer to have them
> at least that faster than more expensive.
> 
> But maybe just my apps are not the best suited for Alphas. That's why it
> would be interesting to hear other's results. But please, don't
> quote any MFLOPS, Spec-Anything, benchmarks etc. I don't believe them :)
> I'm really only interested in *real* apps solving *real* problems.
> The same software takes this-and-this on PentiumIII-750 and
> that-and-that on Alpha 21264/750. This kind of things, so that we do not
> enter long thread of "Alpha is better than Intel. No, Intel is better"
> 
> regards, Michal.
> 
