In article <Xns90356C5A8nobodynowherecom@16.121.112.222>, nobody@nowhere.com (Marble Head) writes:
>
>Does this imply that programs compiled on Tru64 are likely to outperform 
>the same program compiled in Linux?

There are some optimization switches available on Compaq's C compiler for 
Tru64 that aren't on the Linux variant (at least that was true last time I 
looked.)  How much of a win they give you I couldn't say.

>I don't have much experience with either one...
>Is Tru64 faster???
>
>How about VMS?

CPU bound code seems to run at about the same speed (plus/minus a few 
percent) on Linux as it does on VMS on DS10s.  I don't have a Tru64 DS10
for comparison.  However, due to file caching on Linux and RMS overhead on
VMS Linux leaves VMS in the dust on typical file IO tasks, by as much as a
factor of 100.  Optimize the RMS paramaters and play other tricks and you
can bring this down to a factor of 10 (but it requires a lot of work to do
so.)  On the other hand, if the power goes the VMS machine will have all
that data on disk and the Linux machine will lose it, as it was only in
file cache.  Bottom line, for small systems doing scientific/engineering
sorts of applications, where restarting a job should the power fail would
not be the end of the world, Linux is clearly a better choice than VMS, and
likely a better choice than Tru64 (depending upon your weighting of
whizbang compiler and OS features versus price/perforance). 

Regards,

