Thanks everyone for your help with the aboot problems. It is a bit more
complex then it seems -- some combination of my hardware is preventing
aboot/srm from reading a kernel off the hardisk. I have a Samsung generic
20 gig IDE disk, a IDE Zip drive, and two IDE CD-ROMs. Before I forget,
it's a AlphaPC 164sx, RedHat 7.0. No SCSI. Booting from a floppy also
failed, although I didn't try it all that much. Reinstalls, many fdisk
changes, and jumpstart CD usage couldn't fix the problem. 

The final fix was to use the jumpstart CD to write one of it's generic
kernels (2.2.16) to the hard disk. I then needed to boot the system using
the vmlinuz kernel on the jumpstart CD and a flag pointing to the hard
disk install. [Note, boot this way with the RedHat install CD prevented me
from accessing the floppy a zip drives, among other things.] Once the
system was booted this way, I used fdisk on a ZIP disk to make a BSD table
and a small ext2 filesystem. I then ran swriteboot to install aboot on the
zip. Finally I coped all of /boot to the zip disk. 

After a restart, I could use aboot from the zip disk with my own kernel
and the system now works fine. Hope this helps someone else in a similar
situation. 

Jake



