This is obviously off-topic in comp.os.linux.alpha, but there have been
enough misinformation on Tru64 posted already. Just to set the record
straight, Tru64 allows for several (all documented) ways of setting the 
speed of the tu* network interfaces. The recommended way of setting the
network interface speed is to use the 'speed NNN' argument of the
ifconfig (e.g. ifconfig tu0 speed 200 for full-duplex FastEthernet).
This parameter is normally propagated from the interface configuration
options in /etc/rc.config. Other configuration files may also be used
to effect this setting.

In the absence of any explicit specification, Tru64 uses the SRM console
setting. SRM parameter ewa0_mode controls the settings of the tu0 interface,
ewb0_mode controls tu1, etc. For those who, like me, uses remote boot
setup, it is preferable to leave the rc.config setting alone, and rely on
the SRM ew?0_mode variables - this leaves one place to screw up the
settings, instead of two. 

Both ways are reasonably well documented, respectively in the network 
administration guide, and in (system-specific) SRM reference guides. 

My apologies for being off-top. Followups set.

/Serge.P

