Pixelised <pixelised@hotmail(dot)com> wrote:
> And to note one more thing, if "nothing" seems to happen after you issue
> swriteboot then all is ok. swriteboot successfully wrote to cylinder 0 on
> your hard drive.

Maybe one more thing should be added here.  The following:

Disk /dev/sda: 64 heads, 32 sectors, 8715 cylinders
Units = cylinders of 1 * 512 bytes


5 partitions:
#       start       end      size     fstype   [fsize bsize   cpg]
  a:        2       342       341       ext2                      
  b:      343      2943      2601       ext2                      
  c:        1      8715      8715     unused        0     0       
  d:     2944      3071       128       swap                      
  e:     3072      8715      5644       ext2

is a listing of a disklabel from a real, working, Linux system which
boots via SRM from this drive /dev/sda.  Note a presence of an "unused"
type partition 'c' which covers all cylinders on the disk.  This
partition is NOT required by Linux and if you will run out of partition
letters you can safely delete it and reuse to create a "data" partition
instead.  Its sole reason for existence is that if this disk would be
ever seen by DU then a presence of this partition is expected (DU does
not have an equivalent of /dev/sda which would allow you access the the
whole disk and it is using a raw device in this partition instead).

A practical upshot of this is that in a presence of such label
the current Red Hat installer will refuse to write a bootblock on this
disk as there is no space outside this partition table and the fact that
'c' is of "unused" type is not checked.  You will also have to tell
'-f 3' to swritedisk to convince it that you know what you are doing.
There could be some other situations when the installer will opt
to be cautious rather then possibly screwing your data; maybe somewhat
overcautious.  In such cases you will have to boot and write this
boot block yourself.

  Michal


