rwgk@my-deja.com wrote:
> 
> System:
> Red Hat Linux release 6.2 (Zoot)
> Kernel 2.2.14-6.0smp on a 2-processor alpha
> 
> On this system, malloc() returns pointers to places in memory that
> are very far apart. For example, one call returns 0x125f77510,
> and the next one 0x20000002010 (the first call only allocates
> 16 bytes or so, the second one about 160000 bytes).

If I remember correctly, Gnu malloc uses brk(2) for small chunks and
mmap(2) for big chunks.  You can change the threshold with
mallopt(M_MMAP_THRESHOLD, <value>).


> Unfortunately, this behavior is causing us problems. For use in
> a Fortran program, we compute the difference between the pointers
> returned by malloc() and a pointer to a Fortran reference array.
> The difference is divided by the size of Fortran INTEGER, and
> that result passed back from C to Fortran as a Fortran INTEGER.
> If the difference is too large for INTEGER, our program crashes.

You're between a rock and a hard place.  Try mallopt(), but even
it it works you'll be sitting on a time bomb.  Otherwise, a quick
fix would be to replace all INTEGER declarations by INTEGER*8,
without bothering at first to check which integers hold memory
offsets.  If some of those integers are in common, expect trouble.
If some are equivalenced, expect big trouble.

Another way would be to write your own malloc replacement and use the
preprocessor to make your C code use it.  The C library (e.g. stdio)
would still use the real malloc, but presumable it doesn't pass
offsets back to Fortran.


> Our program is very large (>100000 lines) and it is practically
> impossible to find and replace all INTEGER that store offsets for
> dynamic memory allocation. Also, the program is running fine
> on another 64-bit architecture (IRIX64). Why does malloc() on
> the Alpha return pointers to places in memory that are so far
> apart? Could this be changed?

On True64, the compilers have a `-taso' flag to force everything in a
32-bit address space, but that requires support from the OS and is not
available on Linux.  Don't know about Irix.

-- 
Pierre Asselin
Westminster, Colorado


