I have, indeed, made boot disks under linux.
Usually, I use this command:

dd if=whatever of=/dev/fd0 bs=18k
The maximum this way is 80 (or 79, not sure which.)
If you end up with over 80, you know something went wrong.
If you end up with *drastically* less than 80, something probably went 
wrong.

Incidentally, you shouldn't need to specify the block size at all; it is 
just there to improve speed, and make it so you know what happened, and how 
far it got.


