Okay, so far so good.  I was lucky in guessing your problems directly.

Here's what you should do:

When you partition your hard drive, you can (if you want) make the whole 
thing into one partition, and another for swap.  For security reasons, it 
isn't advisable, but you should know that there's no worry about a boot 
partition.
Again, I emphasize, leave the first cyl unused.  Two if you want to be 
generous.

Some SRM consoles don't have the driver built in, enabling the computer to 
boot from an Adaptec 2940 host.  That is why the SRM cannot see the disk.  
It has nothing to do with the disklabel.

Regardless of the disklabel, the SRM should be able to see the disk.  That 
is, if the SRM has the driver for your scsi host.  The disklabel only comes 
into effect when the SRM tries to *read* the disk.  i.e. when you issue the 
boot command.  I believe the disklabel is necessary for booting, but not 
necessary for "sho dev d"

If you are unable to access the 2940 and its disks, after updating your SRM 
firmware, there are a few things you can do:
    	- Attempt boot from ARC.  I've heard that sometimes this is possible, 
the ARC has the 2940 driver and the SRM doesn't, sometimes.
    	- Attach some disk to the motherboard's integrated scsi bus, and boot 
from that disk instead.  (The OS will have the 2940 driver, so the disks 
will become available once you boot the OS).
    	- If above isn't possible, boot SOME other device.  Floppy, CD, 
network, whatever.  Get the kernel to start, and you'll have the software 
driver for 2940 available.

Here are some tidbits that might be applicable:

<snippet from FAQ>
-Q- Where can I obtain firmware updates for the SRM, ARC, or AlphaBIOS?
-A- As of Dec, 2000, go to http://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/
Alternatively, this is how I found the above link:
Go to www.compaq.com. 
Go to Support, Software and Drivers, Alpha Firmware, and select your 
machine.

-Q- How do I change from ARC to SRM, or vice-versa?
-A- If you're in SRM going to ARC, type in
	>>> set os_type nt
	Power cycle your system.
If you're in ARC going to SRM, do the following:
	From the boot menu, select "Supplementary menu"
	Select "Set up the system..."
	Near the bottom, select "Switch to OpenVMS or Digital UNIX console"
	It says your boot selection is currently NT.  
	Select either UNIX or VMS, it doesn't matter too much
	It says your boot selection is currently... whatever you chose
	Select "Setup menu..."
	You will be prompted to power cycle your system.

-Q- How do I change to AlphaBIOS?
-A- This answer will be very vague, as I have not the ability to do it 
myself.
From SRM, type alphabios and press enter.  Only works on some machines.
From ARC, I don't know.
Why would you use AlphaBIOS?  I don't know either.
</snippet>


