>
>NO, nothing, it is normal. In SRM you must boot with SCSI or Floppy.

Not 100% true.

I don't know which machines or firmwares work, but in some cases, the IDE 
device can be used by SRM.

It doesn't have to be dqa.
The driver (I believe) emulates scsi for the ide, so the drive appears as a 
dka scsi device, and you'd never know (from srm) that it's not.

If you're seeing dka devices, count how many you see, and count how many 
disks you have.  You might actually be seeing the ATA disk there.

So the statement "you must boot with SCSI or Floppy" is right, but the way 
around it is to emulate scsi through the driver.


