On Sun, 31 Dec 2000 22:27:33 -0600, Paul Foster <pfoster@execpc.com> wrote:
>
>What would be the best way to put a CD-RW on a UP2000?  Replace the
>normal CD-ROM with any IDE CD-RW that is listed on the cdrecord
>compatibility list or is there a better solution?

My UP2000 has been doing well with a Yamaha CRW8424S SCSI CD
writer.  I had trouble when I tried to add a SCSI CD reader to
the system, but advice from the fine folks at Harddata solved
that.

My working configuration consists of a 68-pin cable between the
UP2000's single-ended SCSI connector, a Plextor PX-40TW (great
drive), the Yamaha CRW8424S through a 68-pin to 50-pin adapter,
and (finally) a 68-pin LVD/SE terminator from CompUSA.  According
to the folks at Harddata, a separate terminator works much better
than the termination built into most drives.  The only downside
to this configuration is I can't boot from the Plextor due to
block size problems.  (I tried with and without the block size
jumper.)  In case it's relevant, I have a pair of Seagate
ST318275LW on the LVD side.

At least with Linux, I'd go with SCSI rather than IDE.  IDE or
maybe just the current IDE drivers are CPU hogs.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com


